<file path="CONTRIBUTING.md">
# Contributing to X-One

Start with [local development](docs/development/local-development.md), create a scoped branch, and keep each change focused. Before review, run the documented environment validator, Compose config validation, relevant lint/tests/build, and update docs in the same patch.

API changes require OpenAPI/tool contracts, typed clients, tests, and API documentation. Database changes require an owned migration, deterministic seed plan when applicable, and data-architecture update. Do not commit secrets, access another service database, bypass hooks, or represent a skeleton as a completed business feature.

Reviewers check service boundaries, idempotency/confirmation on writes, safe errors, least-privilege environment mappings, migration ownership, test evidence, and documentation synchronization.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0001-nestjs-central-orchestrator.md">
# ADR-0001: NestJS is the central orchestrator

Status: Accepted

## Context

Clients need one stable public backend while workflows span conversational and domain concerns.

## Decision

NestJS API Gateway owns public APIs, workflows, confirmation, payment sandbox, audit, and domain adapters.

## Consequences

Domain services stay independent; the gateway becomes the orchestration boundary.

## Alternatives considered

Direct frontend-to-service calls; letting the AI agent orchestrate domains.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0002-logical-database-ownership.md">
# ADR-0002: Each service owns a logical database

Status: Accepted

## Context

One local PostgreSQL container is convenient, but shared schemas couple services.

## Decision

Use a database/user per service with no cross-database queries or foreign keys.

## Consequences

Services communicate through contracts; later deployment can split database instances.

## Alternatives considered

One shared schema and shared database credentials.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0003-synchronous-rest.md">
# ADR-0003: Use synchronous REST for the demo

Status: Accepted

## Context

The foundation needs inspectable, low-operational-complexity communication.

## Decision

Use REST/JSON/OpenAPI with bounded timeouts, correlation IDs, and idempotency.

## Consequences

No queue, broker, event sourcing, or CQRS framework is introduced now.

## Alternatives considered

Kafka, RabbitMQ, and asynchronous event choreography.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0004-one-agent-tool-gateway.md">
# ADR-0004: One agent and a NestJS Tool Gateway

Status: Accepted

## Context

LLM reasoning should not directly mutate authoritative systems.

## Decision

One FastAPI/LangGraph agent produces structured tool requests; NestJS validates and executes them.

## Consequences

Side effects are auditable and confirmation-controlled; no multi-agent system is needed.

## Alternatives considered

Direct agent-to-domain calls and multi-agent orchestration.
</file>

